Yes. I would simplify the theory considerably. For a non-technical reader, the story should be:

**BitMEX did not merely “go offline.” The problem was that traders could not reliably control their positions while BitMEX’s market and risk systems could continue operating. In a highly leveraged market, that meant a customer could be unable to cancel, reduce, hedge, or add margin while the exchange could still move the position toward liquidation. Other exchanges—and BitMEX itself in other incidents—used safeguards designed to prevent exactly that kind of one-sided outcome.**

That is the narrative I would use to justify discovery.

## A simple six-part argument

### 1. Traders were effectively locked out while the market kept moving

BitMEX acknowledged that on March 13, 2020 the DDoS attack delayed or prevented requests going to and from the platform. It also said that although the trading engine continued operating, the authentication/access layer became so impaired that customers found it nearly impossible to reach that engine. ([BitMEX][1])

For a layperson:

> **Imagine your broker can still sell your position automatically, but you temporarily cannot reach the broker to close the position yourself.**

That is the core problem.

The issue is not merely that the website was unavailable. The issue is whether **BitMEX's automated liquidation machinery remained capable of acting against customers at a time when those customers did not have a comparable ability to protect themselves.**

That is exactly what discovery should determine.

---

### 2. Protecting customers during outages was not a new or unusual idea

Years before March 2020, BitMEX itself had responded to system problems by **suspending trading**.

During a July 2017 technical failure, BitMEX suspended trading for more than four hours. Its postmortem says it scheduled the suspension and gave traders notice before stopping the engine. ([BitMEX][2])

So BitMEX already understood:

> **When the trading platform cannot operate normally, one possible risk-control response is to stop trading rather than allow an impaired market to continue.**

[BitMEX — Postmortem: Downtime, July 5, 2017](https://www.bitmex.com/blog/postmortem-downtime-july-5-2017?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

That helps answer a potential defense that there was simply “nothing BitMEX could do.”

There plainly were operational alternatives.

---

### 3. Other crypto exchanges were already protecting leveraged traders during system outages before March 2020

This is probably your strongest industry-practice evidence.

#### Kraken — January 2018

After Kraken experienced a major system-upgrade outage, it did several things before returning to normal leveraged trading:

* cancelled all open orders;
* returned funds tied to those orders;
* **paused margin liquidations for at least 48 hours**;
* disabled creation of new margin positions for at least 48 hours;
* warned customers about the risks of restarting an illiquid market. ([Kraken Blog][3])

That is extremely useful.

A layman's version:

> **Kraken did not simply turn the system back on and let leveraged customers immediately face liquidation. It gave them protection while the market normalized.**

[Kraken — Returns with Free Trading, January 13, 2018](https://blog.kraken.com/news/kraken-returns-with-free-trading?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This supports an argument that by 2018 a major cryptocurrency venue understood that **margin liquidation during or immediately following a platform interruption could unfairly harm customers.**

---

### 4. Binance was already using “cancel before trading resumes” procedures

This comparator is particularly strong because it closely resembles what BitMEX ultimately did in May 2020.

In June 2018, Binance completed a system upgrade but **did not immediately resume trading**. It announced a future trading-resumption time and allowed customers to cancel orders before normal trading restarted. ([Binance][4])

[Binance — System Upgrade Complete, June 27, 2018](https://www.binance.com/en-BH/support/announcement/detail/360005481092?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Then, in May 2019—almost a full year before the BitMEX March event—Binance explicitly announced that after maintenance it would provide customers a **30-minute window to cancel open orders before trading resumed**. ([Binance][5])

[Binance — System Upgrade Notice, May 14, 2019](https://www.binance.com/en/support/announcement/detail/360028054052?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

This gives you a very straightforward argument:

> **By 2018–2019, another major crypto exchange was already using a controlled-reopening process in which customers were given an opportunity to cancel orders before ordinary trading resumed.**

That is remarkably similar to BitMEX's May 2020 **Market Suspended / Cancel-Only** procedure.

---

### 5. Deribit shows that exchanges recognized a responsibility to address wrongful liquidations caused by their technology

Deribit's October 31, 2019 event is a different type of failure, but it is valuable.

A bug in Deribit's BTC index produced an erroneous price movement and triggered multiple invalid liquidations.

Deribit publicly acknowledged:

> the problem was caused by its system.

It then restored affected accounts and reimbursed more than **150 BTC, approximately $1.3 million**. ([Deribit Insights][6])

[Deribit — Flash Crash: A Letter from the Founders, November 1, 2019](https://insights.deribit.com/exchange-updates/deribit-flash-crash-a-letter-from-the-founders/?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

Your argument should **not** be:

> "Deribit reimbursed traders, therefore BitMEX legally had to reimburse traders."

Instead:

> **Before March 2020, another major crypto derivatives venue had already recognized that where exchange technology creates an unfair liquidation result, the venue can take corrective action rather than leave the entire loss with the customer.**

That supports the broader idea that **protecting customers from exchange-caused liquidation harm was an understood industry concern.**

---

### 6. BitMEX itself later adopted essentially the safeguard you say was missing in March

This is the comparison I would put in front of a judge, arbitrator, expert, or nontechnical reader.

On May 19, 2020, BitMEX suffered another outage.

This time BitMEX says:

* **no liquidations occurred while the exchange was offline;**
* the trading platform first reopened in **Market Suspended and Cancel-Only mode**;
* customers were told when full trading would resume;
* ordinary trading resumed only afterward. ([BitMEX][1])

BitMEX also said its reopening procedures had been changed following review of previous downtime events and customer feedback. ([BitMEX][1])

[BitMEX — Postmortem: Downtime, May 19, 2020](https://www.bitmex.com/blog/postmortem-downtime-19-may-2020?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

In plain English:

> **In March, customers allegedly could not reliably manage their positions while the market continued operating. Two months later, when BitMEX had another outage, it protected customers by preventing liquidations, reopening in cancel-only mode, giving customers time to manage orders, and only then restarting trading.**

That comparison does **not by itself prove negligence**.

But it creates a very reasonable discovery question:

> **If BitMEX knew how to prevent liquidations and provide a controlled cancel-only reopening in May, what safeguards existed in March, why were they not activated, and when were the May procedures designed or approved?**

That is exactly the kind of question discovery is meant to answer.

