# The strongest pre-March-2020 comparator events

I would prioritize these four.

| Event                              | What happened                                       | Protective response                                                                            | Why it helps                                                                                                                           |
| ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **BitMEX — July 2017**             | Technical failure / prolonged downtime              | Trading suspended                                                                              | Shows BitMEX itself had previously halted trading during system problems. ([BitMEX][2])                                                |
| **Kraken — Jan. 2018**             | Major trading-system outage/upgrade                 | Orders cancelled; margin liquidations paused ≥48 hours; new margin disabled                    | Very strong evidence that a crypto venue recognized that leveraged traders needed protection after impaired access. ([Kraken Blog][3]) |
| **Binance — June 2018 / May 2019** | Maintenance/restart                                 | Trading delayed while customers were allowed to cancel orders; 30-minute cancel window in 2019 | Strong precedent for controlled restart and cancel-before-trading procedures. ([Binance][4])                                           |
| **Deribit — Oct. 2019**            | Exchange technology caused invalid BTC liquidations | Traders reimbursed; affected equity restored                                                   | Demonstrates pre-March-2020 recognition that exchange-created liquidation harm warranted remediation. ([Deribit Insights][6])          |

Of these, **Kraken January 2018 may be the most powerful industry comparator** for your particular theory.

Why?

Because it deals directly with **margin liquidation**.

Kraken could have simply reopened its system and allowed its automated margin rules to operate. Instead, it paused margin liquidations for at least two days.

That speaks directly to the principle you are trying to establish:

> **When users have just been denied normal access to a leveraged trading platform, it may be inappropriate to immediately allow automated liquidation mechanisms to operate against them before they have a reasonable opportunity to regain control of their positions.**

---

# Put the whole argument into one simple story

For a nontechnical reader, I would present it approximately this way:

> On March 12–13, 2020, Bitcoin experienced extraordinary volatility. This was exactly the time when leveraged traders most needed reliable access to their accounts. BitMEX has acknowledged that during the disruption customer requests were delayed or prevented and that users could find it nearly impossible to reach the trading engine. At the same time, BitMEX reported that its trading engine remained operational.
>
> This created a potentially unfair situation: the exchange's automated systems could continue operating while customers could not reliably control their own positions.
>
> The significance of this becomes clearer when compared with how other cryptocurrency exchanges had handled system interruptions before March 2020. Kraken paused margin liquidations for at least 48 hours after a major January 2018 outage. Binance repeatedly provided customers an opportunity to cancel outstanding orders before trading restarted after maintenance. Deribit compensated traders whose positions were improperly liquidated because of an exchange-system failure in October 2019. BitMEX itself had previously suspended trading during technical problems.
>
> Most importantly, when BitMEX suffered another outage only two months later, on May 19, 2020, it followed a materially different procedure: no liquidations occurred while the exchange was offline, the market reopened first in a suspended and cancel-only state, customers were notified when trading would resume, and ordinary trading restarted only afterward.
>
> These events do not by themselves prove that BitMEX was legally negligent on March 13. They do, however, provide a substantial basis to investigate whether reasonable protective mechanisms existed or were feasible, whether BitMEX considered using them, why they were not used, whether automated liquidations continued while customers could not access their accounts, and whether BitMEX's later procedures were developed in response to problems exposed by the March incident.

That is the kind of statement I think can justify **discovery without overstating what you have already proven.**

# Then tie every argument directly to discovery

Your discovery request becomes much more compelling if you say you are trying to answer five specific factual questions:

**1. Did BitMEX know customers could not reach the trading engine?**
Request incident logs, API metrics, login/OTP failures, customer-support complaints, monitoring alerts and internal communications.

**2. Did liquidation systems continue functioning while customers were locked out?**
Request Liquidation Engine logs, Mark Price records, liquidation trigger records, account-level margin calculations and execution records.

**3. Could BitMEX have suspended liquidations or trading?**
Request March 2020 operational procedures, emergency controls, suspension functionality, prior outage procedures and system architecture.

**4. Why was May 19 handled differently?**
Request documents showing when Cancel-Only procedures were designed, tested and approved; meeting minutes; engineering tickets; incident-response changes; and any documents referring to lessons learned from March 13.

**5. Was BitMEX already aware this type of event could happen?**
Request materials concerning earlier outages, previous DDoS activity, capacity testing, stress testing, load-shedding, access-layer failures and the February 2020 attack activity BitMEX later described.

This is where your argument can become significantly stronger.

You are not asking the court to accept at the discovery stage that:

> **"BitMEX was negligent."**

You are showing that there is enough objective evidence to ask:

> **"Why did BitMEX allow one side of the risk-management relationship—the exchange—to continue exercising control while the customers allegedly could not, when protective alternatives were already known and used in the industry?"**

That is a much more compelling basis for obtaining the internal records you need.

If you're building this into the BRIC report, the next useful deliverable would be a **2–3 page “Basis for Further Discovery” section**, written in nontechnical/legal-investigative language, with the Kraken, Binance, Deribit, pre-2020 BitMEX, March 2020 BitMEX and May 2020 BitMEX events laid out chronologically with footnotes.

